Random Forest benchmark) were trained to identify which vehicle attributes most
reliably move price up or down -- all while holding other factors constant.

Key Takeaways for Inventory & Pricing Decisions:

1. Age is the single strongest price driver. Every additional year of vehicle age is
   associated with a substantial, consistent drop in price.

2. Mileage matters -- but its effect compounds with age. The price penalty for high
   mileage is steeper on newer cars than on older ones.

3. Manufacturer brand tier has a large, persistent effect. Premium/luxury marques
   command a premium; budget/economy brands sit at a discount.

4. Condition and title status are strong, actionable signals. "Excellent"/"like new"
   condition and clean titles carry meaningful premiums.

5. Drivetrain and fuel type carry notable premiums. 4WD and diesel vehicles price
   higher than FWD/RWD and gas/other, respectively.

6. Vehicle body type matters for positioning inventory. Pickups/trucks/SUVs tend to
   price higher than sedans/hatchbacks/coupes of comparable age and mileage.

7. Missing/unknown attribute levels are themselves informative -- incomplete listings
   tend to sell at different (generally lower) price points.

Recommended Next Steps:
- Use the fitted model's predicted price as a pricing sanity-check tool.
- Prioritize sourcing vehicles with the strongest positive price drivers identified
  above, subject to acquisition cost.
- Track model performance over time and refresh training data periodically.
- Consider interaction effects between manufacturer and type, and regional price
  variation, as follow-up analysis.
